        comment     2026-11-15T17:01:46Z
            Built: the chained `Story.app`, `Fake.server`/`call`/`control`/`world`/`Lost`/`storage` and
            `inspect(runtime)` over the old run engine; checks 1 to 11 and 13 green (faults 28 of 29, the dead core
            fails every new Story test); Pay passes across a reload with no Runtime change, so no stop. Decision
            (agent): requirements are typed, not cast: a bound contract carries its commit's `R` as a phantom, `app`
            keeps its machines' types, and `runtimeSetup` requires a services Layer exactly when the contracts
            list services.

            Decision (agent): `inspect(runtime)` lanes keep `operation` (the definition's name, as addresses use it) and
            add `contract` and `state`; the Shape's `operation: "Bank/transfer"` is read as the contract id. Reverse:
            rename. Decision (agent): `Fake.call` takes `failure` always (`Schema.Never` for none), so no default needs
            a cast. Decision (agent): a server is an Effect of `FakeWorld` with each endpoint's words and each control's
            word as its properties; an endpoint named like an Effect member would shadow it (doc Gotcha). Reverse: a
            separate key. Decision (agent): Pay's setup is real: `fixtures/pay/shop-http.ts` is a minimal production
            adapter (200 or NoResponse) so its setup has a services Layer; a Story never builds it (story test). Outside
            the May change list. Decision (agent): static checks (types, not exported) sit inside tests that also read
            the core, so the dead core fails each; the bank contract test checks the fake alone and stays green on a
